From: T0biii <5702338+T0biii@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 20:26:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] patches: openwrt: backport mmc sdio UHS init fixes

Add an OpenWrt patch that backports the pending linux-mmc series
"mmc: core: sdio: fix UHS init with fixed 1.8V vqmmc" (v3) to the 6.6
kernel, so SDIO Wi-Fi modules that misreport S18A at a fixed 1.8V bus
(RTL8822CS on the NanoPi R2S Plus) come up in SDR104 instead of failing
the voltage switch.

Link: https://patchwork.kernel.org/series/1197209/
Signed-off-by: T0biii <5702338+T0biii@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 ...-UHS-init-fixes-for-fixed-1.8V-vqmmc.patch | 185 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 185 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 patches/openwrt/0033-kernel-backport-mmc-sdio-UHS-init-fixes-for-fixed-1.8V-vqmmc.patch

diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0033-kernel-backport-mmc-sdio-UHS-init-fixes-for-fixed-1.8V-vqmmc.patch b/patches/openwrt/0033-kernel-backport-mmc-sdio-UHS-init-fixes-for-fixed-1.8V-vqmmc.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3c86759
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0033-kernel-backport-mmc-sdio-UHS-init-fixes-for-fixed-1.8V-vqmmc.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,185 @@
+From 4c655e7763eeefed490336eec9e16f011cc50599 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: T0biii <5702338+T0biii@users.noreply.github.com>
+Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 20:26:44 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH] kernel: backport mmc sdio UHS init fixes for fixed 1.8V vqmmc
+
+Add the pending linux-mmc series "mmc: core: sdio: fix UHS init with
+fixed 1.8V vqmmc" (v3, not yet merged) to the generic 6.6 kernel:
+
+  mmc: core: sdio: retry without UHS if UHS init fails
+  mmc: core: sdio: skip CMD11 if signalling is already 1.8V
+
+SDIO cards that misreport S18A = 1 while the bus is already at 1.8V
+(e.g. the RTL8822CS Wi-Fi module on the NanoPi R2S Plus) otherwise fail
+the CMD11 voltage switch and fall back to 50 MHz instead of SDR104, or
+do not initialise at all.
+
+Link: https://patchwork.kernel.org/series/1197209/
+Signed-off-by: T0biii <5702338+T0biii@users.noreply.github.com>
+---
+ ...-retry-without-UHS-if-UHS-init-fails.patch | 60 +++++++++++++
+ ...-CMD11-if-signalling-is-already-1.8V.patch | 85 +++++++++++++++++++
+ 2 files changed, 145 insertions(+)
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-1-mmc-core-sdio-retry-without-UHS-if-UHS-init-fails.patch
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-2-mmc-core-sdio-skip-CMD11-if-signalling-is-already-1.8V.patch
+
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-1-mmc-core-sdio-retry-without-UHS-if-UHS-init-fails.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-1-mmc-core-sdio-retry-without-UHS-if-UHS-init-fails.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000..bc0a2a2
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-1-mmc-core-sdio-retry-without-UHS-if-UHS-init-fails.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
++From 27727a85d53afa7af9fbd78ebeafab18f023e0ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Deomid rojer Ryabkov <rojer@rojer.me>
++Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:22:23 +0300
++Subject: [PATCH 1/2] mmc: core: sdio: retry without UHS if UHS init fails
++
++If mmc_sdio_init_uhs_card() fails, e.g. because tuning finds no
++working sample phase, card initialisation fails and the card is not
++usable at all, even though it would work fine in high-speed mode.
++
++This happens for example on the FriendlyElec NanoPi R2S Plus when the
++SDIO pins of the RTL8822CS Wi-Fi module are left at their default 4 mA
++drive strength:
++
++  dwmmc_rockchip ff5f0000.mmc: All phases bad!
++  mmc2: tuning execution failed: -5
++  mmc2: error -5 whilst initialising SDIO card
++  [... repeated for every init frequency ...]
++  mmc2: Failed to initialize a non-removable card
++
++There is already a fallback for a failure to read the CCCR in UHS mode:
++reset the card and restart the init sequence without R4_18V_PRESENT.
++Do the same when UHS init fails, so that the card comes up in
++high-speed mode instead:
++
++  mmc2: UHS init failed (-5), retrying without UHS
++  mmc2: Skipping voltage switch
++  mmc2: new high speed SDIO card at address 0001
++
++The fallback is taken at most once, as the retry doesn't request UHS.
++
++Reviewed-by: Shawn Lin <shawn.lin@rock-chips.com>
++Signed-off-by: Deomid rojer Ryabkov <rojer@rojer.me>
++---
++ drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c | 10 ++++++++--
++ 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
++
++diff --git a/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c b/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
++index 2c58df6..de3686b 100644
++--- a/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
+++++ b/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
++@@ -867,8 +867,14 @@ try_again:
++ 	/* Only if card supports 1.8v and UHS signaling */
++ 	if ((ocr & R4_18V_PRESENT) && card->sw_caps.sd3_bus_mode) {
++ 		err = mmc_sdio_init_uhs_card(card);
++-		if (err)
++-			goto remove;
+++		if (err) {
+++			pr_warn("%s: UHS init failed (%d), retrying without UHS\n",
+++				mmc_hostname(host), err);
+++			mmc_sdio_pre_init(host, ocr_card,
+++					  oldcard ? NULL : card);
+++			retries = 0;
+++			goto try_again;
+++		}
++ 	} else {
++ 		/*
++ 		 * Switch to high-speed (if supported).
++-- 
++2.50.1.windows.1
++
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-2-mmc-core-sdio-skip-CMD11-if-signalling-is-already-1.8V.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-2-mmc-core-sdio-skip-CMD11-if-signalling-is-already-1.8V.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000..e7c53a7
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/459-2-mmc-core-sdio-skip-CMD11-if-signalling-is-already-1.8V.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,85 @@
++From 2505fee7c7b422b43b35a4382df9678f47d2ce6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Deomid rojer Ryabkov <rojer@rojer.me>
++Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:22:24 +0300
++Subject: [PATCH 2/2] mmc: core: sdio: skip CMD11 if signalling is already 1.8V
++
++Per the SDIO Simplified Specification Version 3.00, section 3.1.2, a card
++whose signalling is already at 1.8V must report S18A = 0 in the R4
++response, and the core relies on that: it only sends CMD11
++(SD_SWITCH_VOLTAGE) when S18A is set.
++
++Some cards don't follow this. The Realtek RTL8822CS (as fitted on the
++FriendlyElec NanoPi R2S Plus, where the M.2 SDIO bus is wired to a fixed
++1.8V vqmmc) answers CMD5 with S18A = 1 even though the bus is already at
++1.8V. CMD11 then times out:
++
++  dwmmc_rockchip ff5f0000.mmc: Unexpected CMD11 timeout
++  mmc_host mmc2: Timeout sending command (cmd 0x10202000 arg 0x0 status 0x90202000)
++  mmc2: new high speed SDIO card at address 0001
++
++and the card ends up in 50 MHz high-speed mode instead of SDR104.
++
++The SWT6621S misreports S18A the same way; there, the failed switch
++leaves the card unable to initialise at all (error -110) instead of
++falling back to high speed.
++
++When vqmmc can't do 3.3V, mmc_set_initial_signal_voltage() has already
++fallen back to 1.8V by the time CMD5 is sent, so the host knows there is
++nothing to switch. Skip CMD11 in that case. ocr keeps R4_18V_PRESENT, so
++mmc_sdio_init_uhs_card() still runs and the card is tuned to SDR104:
++
++  dwmmc_rockchip ff5f0000.mmc: Successfully tuned phase to 64
++  mmc2: new ultra high speed SDR104 SDIO card at address 0001
++
++Hosts that start at 3.3V are not affected, nor are compliant cards that
++report S18A = 0 at 1.8V. A non-compliant card that does need CMD11
++before entering UHS at 1.8V would now fail UHS init; with the previous
++patch it falls back to high-speed mode and keeps working.
++
++This relies on the fixed 1.8V supply being described as vqmmc-supply.
++vqmmc is optional: without it, the host can't tell that the bus is
++hardwired to 1.8V. Setting 3.3V then "succeeds", the core believes the
++bus is at 3.3V and CMD11 is still sent, so the patch doesn't help. Such
++boards need to describe the supply (e.g. a fixed 1.8V regulator as
++vqmmc-supply) to benefit from it.
++
++FriendlyElec's BSP for this board works around this with a card
++quirk, MMC_QUIRK_BROKEN_ROCR_S18A[1], matched on the "realtek,rtl8822cs"
++compatible, which drops the switch request whenever this chip is
++present. The misreported S18A is the card's fault, but ignoring it is
++only correct when the bus is already at 1.8V: on a board with a
++switchable vqmmc the same chip would then be run in UHS at 3.3V. A card
++quirk would need knowledge of the board's supply that it doesn't have.
++The core does know the current signal voltage, so handle it there, which
++also takes care of any other card that turns out to behave the same way.
++
++[1] https://github.com/friendlyarm/kernel-rockchip/commit/aee62cf7ae16b5ede6c6d92a8ee20e796482754e
++Cc: Jensen Huang <jensenhuang@friendlyarm.com>
++Reviewed-by: Shawn Lin <shawn.lin@rock-chips.com>
++Tested-by: Longjian Lin <llj@rock-chips.com>
++Signed-off-by: Deomid rojer Ryabkov <rojer@rojer.me>
++---
++ drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c | 6 ++++--
++ 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
++
++diff --git a/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c b/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
++index de3686b..e76b5f7 100644
++--- a/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
+++++ b/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
++@@ -737,9 +737,11 @@ try_again:
++ 	 * 1.8v, the card sets S18A to 0 in the R4 response. So it will
++ 	 * fails to check rocr & R4_18V_PRESENT,  but we still need to
++ 	 * try to init uhs card. sdio_read_cccr will take over this task
++-	 * to make sure which speed mode should work.
+++	 * to make sure which speed mode should work. Some cards (RTL8822CS)
+++	 * set S18A regardless, so don't send CMD11 if we are already at 1.8V.
++ 	 */
++-	if (rocr & ocr & R4_18V_PRESENT) {
+++	if ((rocr & ocr & R4_18V_PRESENT) &&
+++	    host->ios.signal_voltage != MMC_SIGNAL_VOLTAGE_180) {
++ 		err = mmc_set_uhs_voltage(host, ocr_card);
++ 		if (err == -EAGAIN) {
++ 			mmc_sdio_pre_init(host, ocr_card, card);
++-- 
++2.50.1.windows.1
++
+-- 
+2.50.1.windows.1
+
-- 
2.50.1.windows.1

